  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**. Here are all the details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf7a \"Yuppy Love\" \u2013 *Only Fools and Horses*, Series 6, Episode 1\n\n**\"Yuppy Love\"** was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, attracting a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Scene\n\nDel Boy (David Jason) accidentally falls through the bar, attempting to lean up against it while telling Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) about what they should do to appear attractive to women.\n\nA barman comes along and lifts up the section that Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down again, leaving a gap \u2014 and this happens behind Del's back without him noticing. Del then says *\"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\"* before leaning back and falling through the gap onto the floor.\n\nThe moment is made even more perfect by Trigger's shocked reaction, looking around for Del, who is firmly on the floor.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar, except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall right over. Sullivan thought it funny for the man's body language, trying to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and a tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways, then going over without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason felt was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot to get because it was difficult not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script for the episode that went up for auction revealed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack happened to be there on the day and John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack (Trigger) later revealed that Jason never did the fall in rehearsal, so his reaction of shock was completely genuine \u2014 he was seeing it for the first time.\n\n---\n\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever. David Jason gets people asking him about that fall all the time, and some never like to talk about anything else, but he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
